      en: "The German Bell Tower on Pohnpei stands as a significant historical remnant of the brief German colonial administration in Micronesia (1899–1914). Erected in 1907 by Capuchin missionaries, the tower was originally part of a grand stone church dedicated to Saint Anthony of Padua. While most of the mission buildings and the church itself were destroyed during the heavy aerial bombardments of World War II, this sturdy bell tower miraculously survived the devastation. Its solid masonry and European design provide a striking visual contrast to the surrounding lush tropical rainforest. Today, the tower is a preserved landmark that serves as a silent witness to the religious and political ambitions of the German Empire in the Pacific. Located near the town of Kolonia, it remains a focal point for visitors interested in exploring the colonial heritage and the dramatic wartime history of the Federated States of Micronesia.",
